{"from": "artifact:western-wall-ashlars", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-13-01-temple-great-stones", "grade": "B", "note": "The standing Western Wall retains its Herodian lower courses, 'built from enormous meleke limestone blocks' (one stone measures 13.55 m long and weighs 250-300 tonnes, per the fetched article) - the great dressed stones of the Temple complex the disciple admires, in the same building and period. It attests the stones and buildings, not the prediction of 13:2."}
{"from": "artifact:western-wall-ashlars", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-western-wall-ashlars"}
{"from": "text:polybius-6-watches", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-13-35-night-watches", "grade": "C", "note": "Polybius (Hist. 6.35-36) describes the Roman army's night watches assigned by lot to sentries - the four-watch night division that Mark's four named times (evening, midnight, cockcrow, morning) follow. Roman military practice, not Judean civilian reckoning; c. 150 BCE."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-berakhot-1-1", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:mk-13-35-night-watches", "note": "Tension: the Mishnaic night is reckoned in watches of a different scheme - the evening Shema runs 'Until the end of the first watch' (Rabbi Eliezer), while the Rabbis set midnight as the limit - a Jewish division of the night that does not match Mark's four Roman-style named divisions. Compiled c. 200 CE and may not describe 30 CE practice exactly."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-berakhot-1-1", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:sefaria-mishnah-berakhot-1-1"}
{"from": "text:mishnah-berakhot-1-1", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:sefaria-berakhot-1-1-first-watch"}
{"from": "claim:mk-13-35-night-watches", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-roman-timekeeping"}
